{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPk5KezGVHbSD6DuAf4X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both web tools are sitting behind a permission prompt that hasn't been granted, so I couldn't verify a single figure. That changes what I can responsibly hand you.\n\n**I'm not going to write this fact sheet from memory.** You asked for printable citations — author, title, publication, year — attached to specific numbers, running as-is under your byline. Tariff figures are exactly the kind of detail I can recall approximately and attribute wrongly, and a citation that looks clean but is off by a year or attached to the wrong claim is worse for you than no fact sheet at all. Your editor would be printing my reconstruction as your reporting.\n\n**The fix is one click:** approve WebSearch/WebFetch and I'll build the whole thing properly — probably 15–20 minutes of work. If you'd rather not, here's what I can give you that's still genuinely useful.\n\n## Where to verify (these sources are real; the figures I'd pull from them are not yet checked)\n\n- **Vary T. Coates & Bernard Finn, *A Retrospective Technology Assessment: Submarine Telegraphy — The Transatlantic Cable of 1866*** (San Francisco Press, 1979). This is the definitive economic study of exactly your subject. Start here; it should carry the tariff schedule directly.\n- **Charles Bright, *Submarine Telegraphs: Their History, Construction and Working*** (London: Crosby Lockwood, 1898). Near-primary, written by an insider, contains rate tables.\n- **Daniel R. Headrick, *The Invisible Weapon: Telecommunications and International Politics, 1851–1945*** (Oxford University Press, 1991). Good for the reduction curve and the competitive pressure behind it.\n- **Simone M. Müller, *Wiring the World*** (Columbia University Press, 2016). Strong on the cable companies' pricing politics.\n- **Bill Burns, atlantic-cable.com.** Not a citable authority on its own, but it reproduces primary documents — company circulars and tariff notices — that you *can* cite directly.\n\n## Three traps to watch for when you check\n\nThese I'll flag because they're structural, not numerical — they're how this specific story gets reported wrong:\n\n1. **\"£20 for twenty words\" and \"$100 in gold for ten words\" are different claims** and get conflated constantly. Establish which side of the Atlantic, which currency, and which minimum-message length before you print a per-word figure.\n2. **Per-word rates depend on whether address and signature were chargeable.** The rule affects the real cost of a working message substantially, and sources differ on it. A \"per word\" number without that qualifier is soft.\n3. **US dollar figures from 1866 need a gold-vs-greenback note.** The greenback was well below par then, so a gold-denominated price was materially steeper than the number suggests to a modern reader.\n\nAlso worth knowing: much of the freely available web writing on this traces back to a small number of secondary accounts citing each other. Two sources agreeing is weak evidence here. I'd want a company circular or a contemporary trade journal behind the opening tariff specifically.\n\nSay the word on web access and I'll get you the verified sheet with real citations — and publish it as a clean page you can hand to your editor."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":353,"cache_read_input_tokens":32022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":353},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6aff1f95-8210-4701-8553-8c12ba75ea54","uuid":"42a1ebbd-57b1-4a11-906f-a510d2599710","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:14:04.522Z","request_id":"req_011CfPk5KJueVvs9KW6XNwL1"}
